  },
  "grammar": {
    "title": "When the action comes back to you",
    "intro": "Some Spanish verbs describe something you do <b>to yourself</b> — waking up, showering, getting up. These are <b>reflexive verbs</b>, and they carry a little pronoun (<b>me, te, se…</b>) that goes right <b>before</b> the conjugated verb. The very first one you already meet on day one: <b>me llamo</b> (my name is).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "pronombres",
        "heading": "The reflexive pronouns",
        "body": "Every subject gets its own pronoun. It sits <b>before</b> the verb, which is conjugated as normal:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "yo",
            "me"
          ],
          [
            "tú",
            "te"
          ],
          [
            "él/ella",
            "se"
          ],
          [
            "nosotros",
            "nos"
          ],
          [
            "vosotros",
            "os"
          ],
          [
            "ellos",
            "se"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Me</b> llamo Ana.",
            "en": "My name is Ana.",
            "note": "literally: I call myself Ana"
          },
          {
            "es": "¿Cómo <b>te</b> llamas?",
            "en": "What's your name?",
            "note": "tú → te"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "llamarse",
        "heading": "llamarse (to be called) — for names",
        "body": "This is how Spanish says names: the verb <b>llamar</b> + the reflexive pronoun. Never drop the pronoun:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "yo",
            "me llamo"
          ],
          [
            "tú",
            "te llamas"
          ],
          [
            "él/ella",
            "se llama"
          ],
          [
            "nosotros",
            "nos llamamos"
          ],
          [
            "vosotros",
            "os llamáis"
          ],
          [
            "ellos",
            "se llaman"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "Él <b>se llama</b> Carlos.",
            "en": "His name is Carlos.",
            "note": "él → se llama"
          },
          {
            "es": "Nosotros <b>nos llamamos</b> García.",
            "en": "Our surname is García.",
            "note": "nosotros → nos"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "rutina",
        "heading": "Daily routine reflexives",
        "body": "Use them for everyday actions. The verb still follows its normal -AR/-ER/-IR ending — just add the pronoun in front:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "despertarse",
            "me despierto"
          ],
          [
            "levantarse",
            "me levanto"
          ],
          [
            "ducharse",
            "me ducho"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Me levanto</b> a las siete.",
            "en": "I get up at seven.",
            "note": "levantarse + time"
          },
          {
            "es": "Ella <b>se ducha</b> por la mañana.",
            "en": "She showers in the morning.",
            "note": "ella → se"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "Reflexive pronouns go BEFORE the verb: me, te, se, nos, os, se.",
      "For names, always use llamarse: Me llamo…, Te llamas…, Se llama…",
      "The verb keeps its normal ending; you just add the pronoun."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "Yo llamo Ana.",
        "r": "Me llamo Ana.",
        "why": "Names need the reflexive pronoun: me llamo, not just llamo."
      },
      {
        "w": "Se llamo Carlos.",
        "r": "Se llama Carlos.",
        "why": "With él/ella the ending is -a: se llama."
      }
    ]
  },
